==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|pament}}, from {{der|en|xno|pavement}} and reinforced by {{der|en|frm|pavement}}; both from {{der|en|la|pavīmentum||paved surface or floor}}, from {{m|la|paviō|pavīre|to beat, to ram, to tread down}}. Morphologically {{suffix|en|pave|ment|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpeɪvmənt/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-pavement.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪvmənt|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|in technical contexts}} A [[paved]] surface; a hard covering on the ground. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=1|passage=The riches of heaven's '''pavement''', trodden gold.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|British English|Pennsylvania|New Jersey|Baltimore}} A [[pave]]d [[path]], for the use of [[pedestrian]]s, located at the side of a [[road]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|sidewalk|footpath|trottoir|q1=American English|q2=Australia, New Zealand and India|q3=Borrowed from French}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=194|volume=II|passage=It was a cold and rainy afternoon as Ethel Churchill sat at the window of their new abode, a house in one of the streets leading from the Strand to the river. It was the day after their arrival, and nothing could well be more gloomy than the view: the '''pavement''' was wet, and a yellow mist obscured every object,...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author=w:Margery Allingham| title=w:The China Governess| chapter=14| url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W| passage=Nanny Broome was looking up at the outer wall. Just under the ceiling there were three lunette windows, heavily barred and blacked out in the normal way by centuries of grime. Their bases were on a level with the '''pavement''' outside, a narrow way which was several feet lower than the road behind the house.}}
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|North America}} A [[paving]] (paved part) of a [[road]] or other thoroughfare; the [[roadway]] or road surface. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Smollett Peregrine Pickle|volume=II|53|text=[H]e attempted to recover his importance, by haranguing upon the Roman highways, when Mr. Jolter desired the company to take notice of the fine '''pavement''' upon which they travelled from Paris into Flanders {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1991|title=Airpower Journal 1911|page=45
|passage=The antirunway munitions are specifically designed to cause maximum destruction to airfield '''pavements'''.}}
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|North America}} The paved part of an area other than a road or [[sidewalk]], such as a [[cobblestone]] plaza, [[asphalt]] schoolyard or playground, or parking lot.
# {{lb|en|architecture}} The interior [[flooring]] of a church sanctuary, between the communion rail and the [[altar]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}

